Make the parent a flex container, then use `align-items` and `justify-content` to center the child in both directions.

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  min-height: 100vh;
  align-items: center; /* vertical centering */
  justify-content: center; /* horizontal centering */
}
```

If you only need to center one item, a full-page container often uses `min-height: 100vh` so it stretches to the viewport height.

Set the parent to `display: flex` and add `place-items: center` to center its direct children both horizontally and vertically.

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  min-height: 100vh;
  place-items: center;
}
```

`place-items` is shorthand for `align-items` and `justify-content`, so this is equivalent to:

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  min-height: 100vh;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}
```

