You can use the built-in 'http' module to create a server. Here's a simple example that listens on port 4173 and responds with JSON data including the requested URL path:

```javascript
const http = require('http');

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
  res.end(JSON.stringify({ app: 'Nexion', path: req.url }));
});

server.listen(4173, () => console.log('Server running at http://localhost:4173'));
```

This server will return a JSON object with the application name and the requested path for any incoming request.

